a silent walk in Dunkirk charity workers  led a commemoration in the northern  French town for at least 27 people who  died trying to cross the English Channel  one year ago most were Kurds from Iraq  including children  so I thought it was really important to  come here and to um to remember what has  happened and what is happening  still  why the tragedy happen though is still  not clear British and French officials  traded blame over why the boat wasn't  rescued but some details have begun to  emerge a French newspaper investigation  has revealed the victims repeatedly  called French Rescue Services with their  location but they were sent no  assistance and even at times seemed to  be mocked  a transcript of some of the calls paints  a chilling picture  help please I'm in the water says an  asylum seeker yes but you're in English  Waters replies a French rescue operator  no no not English Waters in French  Waters please come fast  then the phone line cuts and the  operator is heard saying oh well if you  can't hear me you won't be saved  despite the danger people keep trying  waiting for a chance in Desperate  conditions on the French Coast Karen  left Afghanistan two years ago he  doesn't want to apply for asylum in  France because he thinks life will be  better in Britain  we have to go of course no one wants to  die but we have to go because we have no  choice  the British and French governments  recently agreed to boost security and  cooperation in France to try and stop  the crossings but Charities say unless  there are more legal ways to reach the  UK women men and children will continue  to risk their lives Natasha Butler Al  Jazeera Dunkirk
 

